              "audio": "media/audio/set3/l1-q17-20.mp3",
              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "So today, let's talk about a really important method archaeologists use called stratigraphy. Basically, stratigraphy is about studying layers of soil and materials at dig sites to figure out when things happened. I mean, the idea is pretty simple—older stuff gets buried deeper while newer things stay closer to the surface. Think of it like a layer cake, where each layer represents a different time period.\nNow, here's how it works in practice. When archaeologists excavate a site, they carefully remove one layer at a time. Let's say they find pottery fragments in one layer and stone tools in the layer beneath it. That tells them the stone tools are older. Another example would be finding Roman coins above Greek pottery—the Greek layer came first, then Romans settled there later.\nBut there are some real challenges with this method. Sometimes natural events mess things up. Earthquakes can shift layers around, or tree roots can push newer objects down into older layers. Flooding can also mix everything together, making it hard to tell what's actually from which period.\nDespite these problems, stratigraphy remains one of our most reliable dating methods. It helps archaeologists build a timeline of human activity at a site and understand how cultures changed over time."
