              "image": "media/pictures/TOEFL Listening Image (Single male 2).webp",
              "audio": "media/audio/set3/l2-q08-11.mp3",
              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "Alright, so you've probably heard the term \"market equilibrium\" thrown around, but what does it actually mean? Well, in economics, equilibrium is basically the point where supply and demand meet. It's where the amount of something people want to buy exactly matches what sellers want to sell at a particular price. When this happens, the market is stable—no shortages, no surpluses.\nLet me give you a concrete example. Imagine coffee shops in your neighborhood. If coffee is priced at five dollars, maybe only fifty people buy it daily, but a hundred people want to sell coffee at that price. That creates a surplus. However, if the price drops to three dollars, suddenly a hundred people want coffee, and exactly a hundred vendors are willing to sell at that price. That's equilibrium.\nNow, here's the thing—markets don't always stay in equilibrium. Lots of factors can disrupt it. Maybe a frost destroys coffee crops, reducing supply. Or perhaps a new study comes out saying coffee is bad for you, which decreases demand. When these disruptions happen, prices have to adjust to find a new equilibrium point.\nUnderstanding equilibrium helps businesses set prices and helps governments make policy decisions. It's one of the foundational concepts in economics because it explains how markets naturally regulate themselves.\nNext, we'll look at what happens when governments interfere with market equilibrium through price controls."
