              "image": "media/pictures/TOEFL Listening Image (Single male 3).webp",
              "audio": "media/audio/set3/l2-q12-15.mp3",
              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "OK, so today we're going to explore something called code-switching.\nThis is when bilingual people switch between two or more languages in a single conversation. It's not random—there are actually patterns to when and why people do it.\nThere are different types of code-switching. One type is called intersentential switching, where someone completes a whole sentence in one language, then switches to another language for the next sentence. For instance, a Spanish-English bilingual might say, \"I'm going to the store. Necesitas algo?\" That's switching between sentences. Another type is intrasentential switching, which happens within a single sentence. The same person might say, \"I'm going to the tienda,\" mixing English and Spanish in one sentence. This type is actually more complex because you need really strong skills in both languages to do it smoothly.\nNow, why do people code-switch? Sometimes it's because a word or concept exists in one language but not the other. Technical terms are a good example. Or maybe they're emphasizing something—switching languages can add emotional weight. Sometimes people code-switch to show group identity, like when immigrants speak one language at home and another at work.\nThe ability to code-switch actually shows sophisticated language control. It's not about being confused or not knowing languages well enough—it's a skill that requires understanding both languages deeply."
